# {{ project_upper }}

A generated general-relativity solver. You write the physics once in Python; the
framework generates, builds, and runs the C++. ({{ evolution_num_vars }} evolution
variables, {{ constraint_num_vars }} constraints.)

## How it works
```
{{ project_name }}.py        the physics: equations, parameters, initial data
   │   python {{ project_name }}.py        (generate)
   ▼
solver/                  generated C++ — kernels + framework glue (don't edit)
   │   cmake --build         (compile)
   ▼
{{ project_name }}Solver run.toml     run a simulation
```

## Quickstart
```
python {{ project_name }}.py
cmake -B build && cmake --build build -j
./build/solver/{{ project_name }}Solver {{ project_name }}_parameters.sample.toml
```

## Where things live
| Want to… | Look in |
|---|---|
| Read/change the equations | `{{ project_name }}.py` |
| Change a run (resolution, time, output) | `{{ project_name }}_parameters.sample.toml` |
| Know what's safe to hand-edit | `CUSTOMIZE.md` |
| Generated C++ (don't edit — regenerate instead) | `solver/` |

New here? Read `{{ project_name }}.py` for the physics and `CUSTOMIZE.md` for the
handful of things you'd ever change by hand. You should never need to touch
`solver/`.
